<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nikos Kazantzakis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1883–1957</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nikos Kazantzakis was a Greek writer and philosopher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. He wrote poetry, plays, travel books and novels, among them Zorba the Greek (1946), Christ Recrucified and The Last Temptation of Christ, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Catholic churches condemned. His epic poem The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odyssey">Odyssey</a>: A Modern Sequel runs to 33,333 lines. He was nominated for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature many times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nikos-kazantzakis.jpg" alt="Nikos Kazantzakis" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nikos_Kazantzakis_1904.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
